WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:04.370
There have been several points over a dozen
seasons of Storage Wars during which bidders

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.160
have genuinely hit pay dirt.

00:00:06.260 --> 00:00:09.880
If you're wondering which abandoned lockers
held the largest treasure troves, here are

00:00:09.881 --> 00:00:12.349
some of the biggest payouts in Storage Wars
history.

00:00:13.349 --> 00:00:17.170
If you search for the biggest recorded find
on the show, chances are one storage unit

00:00:17.170 --> 00:00:18.800
is going to show up every time.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:23.260
The unit was purchased way back in Season
3 by none other than Darrell Sheets, the gutsy

00:00:23.260 --> 00:00:26.580
gambler whose presence has been a staple on
the show over the years.

00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:31.840
Sheets spent $3,600 on a locker that looked
interesting, but not particularly special.

00:00:32.180 --> 00:00:36.020
Imagine his surprise when he discovered that
it was home to a whole slew of paintings by

00:00:36.020 --> 00:00:37.280
Frank Gutierrez.

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:39.100
"Jackpot, baby."

00:00:39.109 --> 00:00:42.829
The proud new owner was floored to find out
that the whole collection was worth upwards

00:00:42.829 --> 00:00:44.580
of $300,000.

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:45.620
"Hold on just a minute…

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.840
300 thousand dollars?!"

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.079
Over the course of the show, the Gambler has
found other ways to make bank besides dealing

00:00:53.080 --> 00:00:53.900
in rare art.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.149
In this case, it came in the form of a toy
and comic book collection that he purchased

00:00:58.149 --> 00:01:00.060
back in Season 5 which he called…

00:01:00.409 --> 00:01:02.199
"The Holy Grail of toys."

00:01:02.199 --> 00:01:06.290
The locker contained an endless supply of
collectible dolls and toys, along with an

00:01:06.290 --> 00:01:11.040
absolutely massive collection of comics: We're
talking 3,000 books.

00:01:11.440 --> 00:01:16.200
The count kept rising as Sheets tallied up
the mountain of collectibles, finally settling

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:18.640
on a total that topped $60,000.

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:24.490
Husband and wife team Rene and Casey picked
up a locker during Season 6 that paid astronomical

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:25.060
dividends.

00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:29.840
As the couple began unloading the treasures,
they discovered an endless procession of high-value

00:01:29.850 --> 00:01:34.770
pieces that ranged from a beautiful grandfather
clock in mint condition to a set of Baroque

00:01:34.770 --> 00:01:39.689
by Wallace collectible silver that was easily
worth at least two grand, along with an oil

00:01:39.689 --> 00:01:45.360
canvas painting that came with its own self-valuing
paperwork stating it was worth $8,000!

00:01:45.749 --> 00:01:50.100
By the time all was said and done, the couple
was practically standing in a museum, and

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.760
the locker was valued at $50,000 minimum.

00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:58.020
While he wasn't particularly remembered for
his huge paydays, Barry Weiss, known as "The

00:01:58.030 --> 00:02:00.780
Collector," was a staple of the show during
its early seasons.

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.280
"Put that in your pipe and smoke it."

00:02:03.420 --> 00:02:07.740
The Collector came through in a big way during
Season 2, when he showed up late to an auction

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:12.430
and quickly bought what appeared to be a worthless
locker with a couch and a few odds and ends,

00:02:12.430 --> 00:02:16.060
all for a seemingly overpriced $1,525.

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:20.230
It turned out that in a small box tucked away
in the back, there was a wooden sculpture

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:24.909
of a person's head with real inset dentures
and glass eyes, along with a window in the

00:02:24.909 --> 00:02:28.680
back of its head that revealed an entire scene
taking place inside the brain.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.580
The rather odd piece turned out to be a bit
of a collectors item and was valued at $6,000.

00:02:34.960 --> 00:02:36.080
"But the problem is…"

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:37.500
"'Cause you're not letting it go.

00:02:38.680 --> 00:02:39.300
Typical Barry."

00:02:39.500 --> 00:02:44.440
Midway through Season 3 of the spin off show
Storage Wars: Texas, Mary, the woman known

00:02:44.440 --> 00:02:49.260
as "The Junkster," got into a disagreement
with her partner, Jenny, over an antique candlemaker.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:54.000
Jenny wanted to sell it for 50 bucks, but
Mary wasn't about to accept that kind of petty

00:02:54.000 --> 00:02:54.500
cash.

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:58.980
She took it to a candle shop where the seller
appraised the unsuspecting little unit at

00:02:58.980 --> 00:03:01.480
an impressive $1,400 at the least.

00:03:02.209 --> 00:03:06.780
While it isn't a shocker as far as the price
tag goes, the piece's estimated price still

00:03:06.790 --> 00:03:11.430
increased nearly thirty-fold in value during
its valuation, which made it one of the more

00:03:11.430 --> 00:03:14.239
unexpectedly valuable treasures to ever air.

00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:17.060
"Today, this bull is a cash cow."

